/** Manual Pitchei Teshuva translations — simanim 066–070 EH001 FULL REDO */
export const PITCHEI_TESHUVA = {
  "066": {
    "1#_": `Only in a time of duress. See Ba'er Hetev and Yeshuot Yaakov s.k. 2, who elaborated and concluded to distinguish: if at the time he accepted kinyan he had leisure to write and the woman could have asked the witnesses to write for her — for we hold "they acquired from him" needs no further consultation — the kinyan helps even not in duress, per Beit Yosef's view; and even if she did not go ask the witnesses, she herself is proof she relied, so kinyan helps like writing even without duress. But if at that time he could not write — such as Shabbat or no leisure — we say she did not rely on kinyan and it helps only like seizing movable property, which helps only in duress. See there.`,
    "2#_": `Kinyan before them. See Ba'er Hetev and responsum of Rama of Pano siman 65.`,
    "3#_": `As large as the first. See responsum Motzal Me'esh siman 25: a woman whose ketubah document was lost and her husband wrote another — the first was ten thousand, the second five thousand, and she did not protest. If the first is later found and she is divorced or widowed — is the first obligation waived, or since she thought it lost but now it is found the first debt still stands? He answered: if the second ketubah does not mention nedunya or addition but plainly writes five thousand because the Sages forbade remaining without ketubah — if the first is later found, the obligation was never waived; only the maneh or two hundred principal was waived, and he wrote the second only for the prohibition, which is only maneh and two hundred. But nedunya and other matters that are a full loan were not waived by writing another ketubah for maneh and two hundred; and the five thousand written — even if maneh and two hundred sufficed — we say he added as Rama wrote. Even if the first is never found, if there is no contradiction and the husband claims the second ketubah waived the first — that is not in his power. But if the new ketubah mentions nedunya and addition and she knew and accepted — certainly the first ketubah is void, even if only a shard is found. So he ruled.`,
    "4#_": `If so nowadays. See Maggid Mishneh ch. 10 Laws of Marriage halakhah 10.`,
    "5#_": `And for a widow a maneh. Dagul MeRevavah wrote: the questioner asks how much is a seduced woman's ketubah — see Tur siman 177, Rambam ch. 1 Laws of Na'arah halakhah 3, and Maggid Mishneh there; and it seems all this when she was a minor, but an adult who was seduced — certainly even per Rambam and Tur she has only a maneh even if the seducer himself marries her. These matters are stated at length in Noda B'Yehuda second edition siman 33; see there and below siman 177 s.k. 3. See also Chasam Sofer end of siman 133: regarding one who marries his own seduced woman I would write two hundred per Tur siman 177 until I found in Dagul MeRevavah that this applies only to a minor, not an adult who has only a maneh — the elder so ruled and so is proper. And there siman 125 regarding one who conceived soon after the wedding and they admit the pregnancy is from him without doubt of another man's nursing — a sage challenged that two hundred was written as for a virgin, and Dagul MeRevavah wrote that if she is an adult she has only a maneh (and thus the ketubah is invalid); he answered it is correct, even though we learn "if he wishes to add he may add" and Ran holds additions may be included in one sum with the ketubah plainly, not like Mordechai — nevertheless Ran agrees one cannot write "that which is fitting for you from the Torah," and Dagul MeRevavah's words are very correct for an adult who has no fine and whose first intercourse was with consent — Rambam also agrees she has only a maneh. Nevertheless in our case there is no concern, for all this is a generally seduced adult who later marries; but for his betrothed with conditions and penalties as customary, her heart was confident she would marry him and knowingly gave herself to him that he would marry her with two hundred like virgin bride-price — certainly she waived only with this understanding. And even if one stubbornly says if this seducer will not write two hundred we cannot compel him — at minimum if he wrote two hundred "that which is fitting for you," how can we say it is not fitting from him, for certainly it is fitting and he is obligated to write so. See there. And what Ba'er Hetev cited from R' Meir Mintz regarding one who raped a virgin and married her — see below siman 143 s.k. 6 for explanation of R' Meir Mintz's words.`,
    "6#_": `And writes more. Ba'er Hetev cited Beit Shmuel s.k. 23 through "and he can profit from them" from Beit Shmuel's language. Sefer HaPela'ah wrote in Kuntres Acharon letter 13: Hagahot Maimoniyot wrote this is not interest because even if he divorces or dies immediately he must give this addition. But per our enactments, in the first and second year he does not give the addition in full until after three years — thus this answer does not apply. See there where he elaborated; see also Hagahot Yad Efraim and in my view no need to elaborate per what is explained above siman 53 in Beit Shmuel s.k. 16 that returning within three years does not apply to nedunya law or one-third addition which is like a nedunya debt and she loses only regarding maneh and two hundred or voluntary addition. In my view these do not suffice to answer the aforementioned Gaon: first, Beit Shmuel's law that one-third addition is like nedunya is not agreed, for in our case it is clear she takes only nedunya not one-third addition as I brought there in his name. Further, Noda B'Yehuda second edition siman 12 wrote if he did not profit on the principal sum Beit Shmuel also agrees — brought there too. Further, the Gaon did not refer to ShUM enactment but another enactment, since he says "only after three years" while ShUM is only the second year; Yad Efraim there also cites "only after three years." Even if "after three" is a mistake for "after two," his language implies an enactment not to give the addition in full, like the custom now in the country I mentioned siman 53 s.k. 14 or Prague custom in responsum A.A.Z. part 2 siman 147 giving half addition — the above answer does not apply. Nevertheless in my view I do not understand Sefer HaPela'ah's difficulty, for all these enactments whether ShUM or ours were enacted lest she remain empty — which applies only to death not divorce, for he divorces willingly; thus Hagahot Maimoniyot properly said it is not interest since even if he divorced immediately he must give this addition — it is not "waiting for payment," and "or if he dies" is incidental, but the main answer is if he divorces her. In Hagahot Ashri ch. Found Object of a Woman siman 4, R' Shmuel explained it is not interest since if he divorced immediately he would have to add; also Turei Zahav on that chapter brought this answer in Mordechai's name; also Beit Shmuel s.k. 16 brought this answer because if he divorces even immediately, etc. — and from omitting "or dies immediately" it appears they intended this; uncertain. See Brit Avraham siman 75 letter 1 who elaborated on Hagahot Ashri's main reason that even if he divorced immediately he must add — he proved in his work Hovat Chen siman 177 that even then it is interest, but concluded per Sefer Halakhot Ketanot it is not interest since perhaps collection will never come, like acquiring her property on doubt that if she dies he inherits, etc. See there. And what Ba'er Hetev further wrote about Krakow custom excluding garments, etc. — see Noda B'Yehuda second edition siman 100 questioned a ketubah stating the groom added one-third of the principal beyond garments and jewelry, doubting whether "belonging to her person" means the husband acquires for her or only what she brought; also that extra language was not used for jewelry as gift but only that she takes jewelry; he answered the necessity that it refers to what she brought is no proof, for there is relevance to two returns siman 53 and moredet siman 77 and taking without oath end of siman 88. On the contrary, it proves the intention is on what the husband does; if on what she brought, why write she takes all this besides ketubah — she would take nedunya besides ketubah even without writing. Beit Yosef Choshen Mishpat end of siman 61 in Rashba's name: we do not interpret extra language for benefit except where they said we have no power to judge imaginings; but siman 42 Beit Yosef brought Maharik who does interpret extra language; and Rashba did not close us off except where the matter itself must be written with only extra language, but where it need not be written at all we say from explicit writing it comes for benefit she would not otherwise deserve (he elaborated; apparently disputed between Tanna Kamma and Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel Bava Metzia 83 — per our ruling like Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel, see Tur Choshen Mishpat siman 231 and Sma there; uncertain. See Bava Batra 67 and 138 and Perishah Choshen Mishpat siman 42 letter 10 where I elaborated). Thus also here the condition she takes applies to her person if the intention is nedunya she brought — entirely superfluous; though one may doubt why he wrote one-third addition for belongings she brought — it is strained; Beit Yosef Choshen Mishpat siman 42: whatever meaning is clear to beit din from the writing, they judge even for the husband's benefit, etc. Regarding the second doubt since he did not mention he added, etc. — in my youth I wondered why they give a widow per ketubah all belonging to her person even what the husband did not have at marriage — by what right, even if written as gift he did not obligate himself to buy jewelry, and post-wedding jewelry is not in his power to give as gift per Choshen Mishpat siman 259. I then justified the custom: since for a widow they assess what is upon her below siman 99 because she did not intend gift when leaving — we can say this is plainly, but where he wrote in the ketubah she takes all belonging to her person, he revealed he is pleased even when leaving she should take; thus when he gives after the wedding he acquires for her and she takes, not by kinyan at marriage. Therefore even inferior language not of gift, he revealed he wants her to take when leaving. Another reason for the custom: per Choshen Mishpat siman 259 s.k. 4 if he seized after it came into existence, etc. — specifically with effective language; thus one may doubt this ketubah. But we need my youth's reason, for if she acquires when it comes into existence — fine per Sma siman 66 s.k. 46 even seizure not before seller; but per Shach only when seller knows, and waiver applies to selling to a stranger, etc. — here if this woman acquires belongings it depends on Sma-Shach dispute; even per Sma my reason alone is straight; therefore hard to completely deny this woman belongings, better to favor the widow. See there siman 101 for further merit that jewelry specifics were listed initially but silver and gold jewelry were added finally — intention is on belongings he will acquire after the wedding; nevertheless he concludes compromise is good. See what he wrote below siman 99 s.k. 1 in name of Givat Shaul.`,
    "7#_": `They write "from the groom's house." So they know she is an orphan — relevant if she dies within the first year as written siman 118. Beit Shmuel and Chelkat Mechokek seif 25: now the widespread custom is also for orphans, etc., and even if not written — if so there is no concern. See Tosafot Shabbat 23b.`,
    "8#_": `They write "the divorced woman." Ba'er Hetev. See Chasam Sofer siman 130 regarding a divorcee's ketubah without "divorced woman": a rabbi went abroad for a month and it was hidden from everyone that Tashbetz part 2 siman 173 wrote they did not practice like Rambam (ch. 4 Laws of Yibum halakhah 33) to write in a divorcee's ketubah "so-and-so the divorcee," etc. He answered this is incorrect — if Tashbetz were before us he would require another ketubah or seizing movable property and writing another ketubah after chuppah, for one examining Tashbetz sees his main reason that one marrying her (if he checks this ketubah and sees she has only a maneh) knows she is a divorcee from the first, for if a widow she would be a killer and forbidden to all, etc. — meaning Rambam per his view is not concerned for killer-wife suspicion but only a mere concern, therefore concerned for priesthood; but per all poskim who are concerned for killer-wife, she has both priesthood and killer concerns, so how blame the woman on her husband. Therefore for us who are very lenient in the manners mentioned siman 9 in the gloss, the concern returns — thus great necessity to mention "divorced woman." And reciprocally, since they already practice as Rama wrote to write "divorced woman," even if Tashbetz himself were before us he would fear if this ketubah comes before a third party he would judge she is certainly not a divorcee since they practice writing "divorced woman" — thus what was done in the case before us is improper, etc. It would seem right to say the ketubah is invalid, not written for this divorcee woman but for a widow — she is another. But Maharam siman 109 wrote nevertheless it is not invalid, for a divorcee is also called a widow, etc., and I say because the Sages called widow after maneh — whoever collects a maneh can be called widow unless "widow" is written (and what he wrote above requiring another ketubah and even without writing widow — when he sensed the mistake before chuppah, as ab initio). See there. And what he wrote "if she had intercourse they write 'had intercourse.'" See Nishmat Adam not to write "had intercourse," so Beit Meir; write only "so-and-so betrothed" and that suffices as a sign. See responsum Radbaz part 1 siman 180: a convert married in ancient days, divorced, now remarries — do they write "the convert" or "the divorcee"? He answered apparently no concern since both are forbidden to priesthood; but on examination it is proper to write "the convert" so conversion is not forgotten, lest tomorrow this one divorces her and she marries a convert and his daughter is unfit for high priesthood per Ra'avad (ab initio when consulting they so instruct, see Kiddushin 48b and Maggid Mishneh siman 7 s.k. 21). Further relevance for marrying her father's relatives — they did not decree on a convert regarding father's relatives at all, and even mother's relatives there is relevance since prohibition is only rabbinic; therefore always write "the convert" so her lineage is not forgotten. Need not write "divorcee" for divorcee and widow are included in convert forbidden to priesthood. And for divorcee and widow write "the divorcee," etc. See there.`,
  },
  "067": {
    "1#_": `They did not enact ketubah for her. Beit Shmuel s.k. 7. See Emet L'Yaakov Avinim ch. 14 Yevamot 41: it is plain to me this is specifically when she married as an adult — her marriage is only rabbinic; but where her father accepted betrothal while she was a minor — her betrothal is Torah law — certainly she has ketubah. Therefore they said in the Gemara ch. Elu Na'arot (Kesubos 37) a deaf-mute woman has virginity claims — meaning married through her father while a minor. Beit Shmuel siman 67 s.k. 7 and Chelkat Mechokek erred. See Maggid Mishneh there and Beit Meir here.`,
    "2#_": `And wrote her a ketubah. See responsum Tzemach Tzedek siman 68; brought below siman 121 seif 6 s.k. 7 with the formula how to write her ketubah.`,
    "3#_": `She has the principal. Ba'er Hetev. And see Beit Meir who disputes and holds other betrothal is required; he wrote: although bedieved if he had intercourse after maturity alone or through seclusion before witnesses, other betrothal is not required and we say a person does not act licentiously as explained siman 31 seif 9 — nevertheless when he comes to ask before intercourse, I have no doubt we instruct him to betroth with money kiddushin not "you are betrothed," as Rav Menagid, etc. See there and Noda B'Yehuda second edition siman 52 brought briefly above siman 43 s.k. 1.`,
  },
  "068": {
    "1#_": `Was from a family. See Ikarei Dinim Yoreh Deah siman 21 letter 10: what people say one born on the sixth day has no virginity blood, and therefore in Pisaro they recorded every girl born on the sixth day in the community register with scribe and one witness as testimony when needed (he brought in name of Pachad Yitzchak letter 70 that this has no basis and is complete falsehood; he wrote the matter is still doubtful, perhaps not only the day's fortune but also the hour's fortune, etc. See there).`,
    "2#_": `{Rama: Some say a claim of open opening, etc.} See Yeshuot Yaakov s.k. 6 who challenged why Rama did not emend here that nowadays he is not believed to say "open opening I found," since there is Rabbenu Gershom's ban against forced divorce, as Rama wrote below siman 178 that he is not believed to say he believes the witness lest his eye turned to another; later rabbis there wrote likewise one saying he saw she committed adultery is not believed, as Mordechai in Kiddushin, etc. Do not say he is believed on open opening because there is a presumption a person does not trouble for a feast and lose — first, that presumption helps only for rabbinic ketubah not to remove a woman from her husband; further Rashi explained if he hated her he would divorce her, and after Rabbenu Gershom's enactment this presumption is hidden. He wrote to answer: "his eye turned to another" applies only where per his words she loses ketubah, such as saying he saw willing adultery — then she has no ketubah; but where he claims open opening, it could be she was raped under him and then she has ketubah and he must divorce and pay — we are not concerned, etc. Or perhaps Rama relied on what he wrote below and concludes whoever finds a place to answer Rama's words otherwise is a great sage and reward from Heaven. See there. I do not understand the first answer that "his eye turned" applies only where she loses ketubah — for below siman 178 seif 9 Rama on Shulchan Aruch there "did not warn her and one witness came, etc., he divorces and gives ketubah" — Rama wrote nowadays not believed, etc., or believes the witness lest his eye, etc. — clearly even where he must give ketubah we say his eye turned. And what he wrote such as saying he saw willing adultery then she has no ketubah — also not understood, for truly the law even saying he saw his wife commit adultery he must give ketubah as Shulchan Aruch siman 115 seif 7, only he can impose oath (and some dispute Chelkat Mechokek and Beit Shmuel there); since we suspect he lies because his eye turned, certainly she swears and he must pay ketubah. Therefore this answer fails and the second is necessary — Rama relied on below, and thus a novel law that nowadays he is not believed to claim open opening to forbid her to him. Even admitting this law, one may say specifically for a kohen's wife; but for an Israelite's wife where the father accepted betrothal under three years, even nowadays he is believed per Rama — one may say in the next seif if she justifies herself saying "I was raped" she is believed; thus we find he is not believed "his eye turned" only when saying he saw adultery or believes or believes the witness — where per his words she is certainly forbidden to him we suspect he lies wanting forced divorce; not here where even per his words she is not certainly forbidden, lest she claim "I was raped" and he cannot force divorce — we do not say his eye turned lest he lie on doubt for benefit. Nevertheless on the main difficulty it seems only per later rabbis he brought that even saying he saw adultery he is not believed — but for Rama himself, he in siman 178 seif 9 gloss did not mention the husband saying he saw adultery, only believing or believing the witness; explained in Noda B'Yehuda second edition siman 11 (brought siman 178 there) that Rama's view when saying she committed adultery all agree she is forbidden; what Hagahot Maimoniyot in Rosh and R' Yehuda's name that one saying his wife committed adultery she is not forbidden — does not mean he saw with his eyes but knows through some excuse; also responsum Mahari ben Lev part 3 siman 226 explains Hagahot Maimoniyot thus. See there. And one claim of open opening — they said in the Gemara it is like two witnesses, like saying he saw with his eyes she committed adultery — Rama also agrees he is believed, therefore Rama did not emend here at all; uncertain in all this.`,
    "3#_": `If he is a kohen. Beit Shmuel. And see responsum Rama of Pano siman 226. And what Beit Shmuel and Chelkat Mechokek further wrote that nowadays they betroth under chuppah, etc., one may say she is not forbidden to her kohen husband. See Me'ir who wrote they shortened where they should have elaborated, for this helps only in a city of all kosher Israelites with no relative disqualifying his priesthood; but in a city with gentiles, disqualified persons, or resident relatives, this law is engraved in Sma end of ch. 6 regarding widow and similar — here she contradicts and does not claim "I had intercourse with a valid man" — she leaves unless with two majorities to validate. See there siman 6.`,
    "4#_": `Behold she is forbidden to him. See responsum Maharival part 2 siman 6 — plain to him since the Gemara said open opening is like two witnesses, beit din compels divorce. See there. But responsum Mahari HaLevi brother of Taz siman 39 wrote the Gemara explanation "believed to forbid her" means we tell him she is forbidden when leaving Israel, but beit din does not compel him; and "open opening like two witnesses" means he must divorce to leave and regarding severity of prohibition they are like two witnesses, but not for beit din compulsion. See responsum Brit Avraham siman 86 letter 2; explained there agreeing with Maharival that beit din compels divorce. See what I wrote siman 115 seif 7 s.k. 37.`,
    "5#_": `Forbidden to him. See Penei Yehoshua on Kesubos there: plain she is permitted to another kohen, only forbidden to himself as a certainty. In Kuntres Acharon siman 24 he left this uncertain for practical law. See Noda B'Yehuda second edition siman 14 brought below s.k. 8; see responsum Teshuvah Me'ahavah part 1 siman 156 doubting if she is permitted to yavam per what is stated Sotah 5b — reason Sotah is forbidden to yavam: since she was forbidden to his brother she is forbidden to yavam who comes through him? Or perhaps distinguish Sotah forbidden to husband from Torah versus certainty which is also Torah but after permitted to another kohen perhaps permitted even to yavam who can say what is his claim to me. See there.`,
    "6#_": `Permitted to him. Ba'er Hetev. Beit Shmuel wrote from this if there was doubt, etc., doubt helps remove money. Beit Shmuel concludes so explained in Tosafot in this view, and so I wrote above in name of responsum Ben Lev and Shach — meaning siman 53 s.k. 9; I already wrote there s.k. 14 many later authorities dispute. Also Perishah on Kuntres Acharon on this sugya siman 34 wrote no proof from Tosafot and ruled not like Beit Shmuel. See there.`,
    "7#_": `{Rama: Some say this that she becomes forbidden.} Chelkat Mechokek and Beit Shmuel. See Noda B'Yehuda second edition siman 21: you challenged that siman 178 seif 3 gloss implies she is not believed to say "I was raped" only because of miggo, but siman 68 seif 7 gloss Rama wrote some say if she said "I was raped" she is believed and there is no miggo. Know the lenient view's reason is establishing her on presumption of fitness; but here seclusion lost presumption of fitness since she did transgress. I found Perishah on Kuntres Acharon Kesubos siman 30 also wrote thus, and I say this is necessary from Hagahot Maimoniyot brought Beit Shmuel s.k. 15: even for a kohen's wife if she said raped before betrothal she is permitted — seemingly wondrous, for if she speaks of before betrothal why say "I was raped" — while still unmarried no difference between rape and consent; or the main reason is presumption of fitness, and when they admit intercourse with consent she lost her presumption and is not believed at all. Therefore I wonder at Chelkat Mechokek s.k. 10 who wrote likewise for a kohen's wife saying raped before betrothal, etc., and so the Maggid — per the lenient view specifically when she says "I was raped" he brings Hagahot Maimoniyot. Nevertheless for practical law Chelkat Mechokeh's words can be upheld, etc. See there and siman 14 where Gaon Or Chadash also challenged this contradiction; his grandfather Gaon Pnei Aharon part 3 (there s.k. following) noticed this and answered no contradiction as said above. See also responsum Brit Avraham siman 78 letter 2, siman 82 letter 2, siman 84.`,
    "8#_": `"I was raped" — she is believed. Even HaEzer ch. 1 Kesubos siman 18; Maggid Mishneh ch. 18 Laws of Forbidden Relations; Beit Yosef here; Beit Shmuel s.k. 15; Perishah on Kuntres Acharon siman 23. See Maggid Mishneh there halakhah 10 who brought Yerushalmi explicitly like the forbidders even saying plainly, not like Rabbeinu Yonah and supporters. Yerushalmi also brought responsum Maharam Alashkar siman 94 supporting forbidders. See there at length also end of siman 77. Nevertheless Noda B'Yehuda second edition siman 14 on a case where her husband went far away about a year and a half, she gave birth and claims raped by a wicked disqualified man while her husband abandoned her in a distant house with only a small daughter — is she permitted to her Israelite husband. He elaborated on Rabbeinu Yonah and Rosh and brought Yerushalmi and wrote no proof from Yerushalmi, for Yerushalmi per its view that rape has voice overriding even certainty and even plain claim does not help; but our Talmud disputes Yerushalmi, for explicitly an Israelite's wife is permitted — certainty maybe not under him maybe rape — if certainty permits without plain claim, permitted even on one doubt (so too responsum Brit Avraham siman 78 letter 3). He further argued Rambam also holds thus, not like Maggid Mishneh, and elaborated concluding if her husband believes her and his heart does not rebuke him as she is presumed modest before him, he may rely on Rabbeinu Yonah and she is permitted. At the end he wrote Knesset Yechezkel brought in Maharam Alashkar's name that "I was raped" — she is believed means saying plainly "I was raped" without naming the adulterer, or even naming him if not present; but if present and contradicts her even against the husband as plain certainty, since the adulterer contradicts with plainness, considered plain against the husband too. He wrote to doubt this, deducing from Gemara R' Chiya: one saying open opening is believed to forbid her — why conclude specifically against himself as plain and plain, but not believed to forbid priests if he died before divorcing her, for regarding other kohanim it is plain and maybe. Thus not like Maharam Alashkar; in any case we need not send after the adulterer since not present we accept the woman's words. He concludes nevertheless for stringency do not rely on my view alone unless two great authorities of the generation agree. See there siman 15: what place Maharam Alashkar has is when that person contradicts saying not by rape but willingly (as the questioning Gaon thought — he answered far to say so, for then Knesset Yechezkel would not omit this). See Maharam Alashkar siman 94 — the case where that person completely contradicted saying never came upon her by rape or consent; per what is explained there Maharam Alashkar did not write this view definitively only "we can say," and he forbade the woman there for other reasons. But one completely contradicting he never had intercourse with her — she is permitted since the adulterer does not contradict her permission whose main permission is rape — we split her statement, etc. Further if the person contradicted before she was permitted, perhaps Maharam Alashkar's words apply; but if already permitted, the adulterer's contradiction does not help even if he says willing intercourse, like captives once permitted, etc. Maharam Alashkar's words are not so clear. See there siman 17 further on this; siman 16: regarding this woman claiming rape, I already informed you, what more shall I add; if the husband's heart does not rebuke and he trusts her righteousness saying she surely does not lie — this is as saying plain to me; good also to investigate neighbors if she is always presumed modest and no licentiousness heard — he may permit her. See siman 16: I am very doubtful regarding a woman saying "I was raped" — Rabbeinu Yonah permits and Rambam forbids as explained Hagahot Maimoniyot ch. 18; I am doubtful even per Rambam she is not believed, but perhaps one witness testifying she was raped is believed to permit her to her husband because one witness is believed regarding prohibitions — specifically regarding the woman's credibility Rambam was stringent; even in Sotah where husband warned and she secluded and one witness testifies she was not defiled — in this contradiction where he peered through a hole and saw she did not commit adultery — certainly not believed to permit; proof from Ulla in Sotah 31 — captive differs since there are grounds for matter he warned and secluded. See there siman 16, 18, 19. The main Noda B'Yehuda words that plainly her heart is between her teeth and husband overseas and she says raped equals open opening law — per Rabbeinu Yonah and supporters permitted when presumption of fitness not lost — see responsum Knesset Yechezkel siman 78: his view is not that any woman whose husband was overseas and she bore illegitimate child saying raped is believed per Rosh and supporters; I saw no posek stated this explicitly; perhaps they relied on open opening where Israelite's wife father accepted betrothal under three years. Truly one may say "I was raped" for two reasons: rape has voice; and from embarrassment she says raped only regarding open opening where father accepted betrothal under three years with miggo she could say seduced as minor without voice or embarrassment. Further distinguish open opening claim — prohibition is not established except per her husband and she immediately says raped — therefore believed, unlike illegitimate child where prohibition was established before she said raped. I remember as a youth in Brisk a doctor came from Italy several years; his wife came pregnant with illegitimate child claiming raped on the road — they did not listen and forbade her to him, and I think he was Israelite, etc. Perishah on Kuntres Acharon Kesubos siman 31 disputes him, writing from these words there is room to permit a woman whose husband went overseas, tarried more than twelve months, she conceived and claims raped on the road — she is believed and permitted to her Israelite husband since there is plain and presumption of righteousness better than majority of consent as Tosafot taught majority consent is not a full majority; since I saw Knesset Yechezkel's case in Brisk where sages of the generation forbade her, I had to write it is not a general rule; and even though we do not answer lions, one may say in that case even per her claim of rape she transgressed by secluding with a warrior in an inn forbidden even with many kosher men until a guard sits — thus where she transgressed seclusion improperly presumption is greatly impaired and no rape claim; not so where she claims rape in a matter that can be established afterward — seems we should not forbid her to her Israelite husband; I say this only to learn, and more Rama (brought above seif 4 s.k. 14) would permit if she claims privately her husband came by name, even for forbidders. Also responsum A.A.Z. part 8 ch. 3 brings also illegitimate pregnant saying raped equals open opening questioned there about a woman whose husband is in another country, she conceived and bore illegitimate child; beit din investigated and she answered raped in sleep on the road from road weariness sleep seized her and she did not sense a man had intercourse; afterward her husband came asking to permit dwelling with her as he believes her rape claim and testifies he saw nothing unseemly since the wedding — he answered at length clarifying two doubts: whether sleep-rape is rape (see above siman 11 Ba'er Hetev s.k. 18); and whether a woman is believed saying raped where she has no miggo. Regarding first doubt — seems if intercourse in sleep she is not forbidden to husband, for we learn ch. 2 Keritot all forbidden relations whether awake or asleep — asleep is exempt; Tur siman 20 and Rama there in Darkhei Moshe and glosses Shulchan Aruch: therefore Tur wrote, etc., relevance nowadays in which intercourse a woman becomes forbidden — thus if she is asleep she is not forbidden. Though one may say Tur is not universal as Beit Shmuel there regarding intercourse with dead limb, and perhaps here too specifically Torah rape but sleep is considered inadvertent not rape; main proof from Darkhei Moshe from Nekudot Yosef is not necessary — Tosafot in Yerushalmi name and Beit Shmuel siman 11 s.k. 3: he is intentional and she inadvertent — she is permitted to husband; so too Talmud Sotah 25, plainly in sleep one may say considered rape, etc. Regarding second doubt if believed saying raped in sleep since no miggo — all know pregnant and husband distant — like witnesses she had intercourse — seems also believed per Rosh ch. 1 Kesubos Tur siman 68, etc.; even Mordechai ch. 2 Kesubos and Shulchan Aruch siman 178 gloss from Mordechai — those Geonim Mordechai brought hold like forbidders rejecting Rabbeinu Yonah; but ultimately Mordechai wrote to uphold Rabbeinu Yonah and permitted regarding open opening even on one doubt even without miggo — therefore wonder at Rama (see previous s.k.) and he elaborated concluding in the questioned case not only beit din need not remove her but even on the husband no obligation to remove since she says plainly raped in sleep by another and husband believes her — husband cleared of sin and may maintain her if sages agree. At end responsum Rabbi Yonatan of Prague wrote not to explicitly permit husband to dwell with her nor compel beit din to remove her; but expert rabbinical court of Krakow agreed permitting husband to dwell with wife. See there (see Chasam Sofer siman 131 on this). See also Knesset Yechezkel there about a woman who became insane while dwelling with her Israelite husband and was sent to a small city far away; per Jewish testimony she was sometimes dreaming and madness grew; husband overseas, rumor she is pregnant, beit din investigated — she cried describing a wagon driver from Lithuania, etc., intercourse in a field, threw stones, returned in madness, later in sanity sensed pregnancy, etc. — her words; husband came horrified asking if permitted; he answered at length (siman 76) possibly to permit even without her words per Rashi and Tosafot on open opening sugya and Ra'avad Sotah and Rif ch. 18 Laws of Marriage that seduction of minor is rape since no consent — so too deranged seduction is rape; all the more minor; if so also without words possible to permit even one doubt though no majority of consent against presumption of fitness — questioned Tosafot there, answered all establish afterward she was minor without voice; here in seclusion during madness no voice; Knesset Yechezkel challenged from this that we establish afterward she was minor and voice of rape applies because not ashamed to publicize — applies also here. But truly Beit Yaakov siman 6 refuted his proof and brought responsum Maharam Lublin rejecting Knesset Yechezkel for another reason; Knesset Yechezkel is lone against all these authorities Maharal, Beit Yaakov, Maharam Lublin, Perishah who all questioned plainly we establish afterward minor without voice. He further vast pilpul to prove not like Knesset Yechezkel — also without her words broad permission not like Rambam because here no voice. See siman 82 end letter 8: cannot retroactively determine when pregnancy recognized two years after act. Nevertheless Knesset Yechezkel's challenge stands if open opening has voice against majority consent — also here without words permission per forbidders of open opening through presumption of fitness; Knesset Yechezkel challenged further; Beit Yaakov siman 6 refuted; Maharam Lublin; he concluded not like Knesset Yechezkel, also without words permission because no voice per open opening forbidders; Rambam ch. 1 holds seduction is not rape; also deranged worse than minor and deaf as explained responsa and Ran Gittin 22b, Chullin 12b, Yevamot 104b, Rashba siman 26 and 493 — open chapter proof does not apply to deranged seduction (see below siman 178 seif 3 s.k. 7 — also inclined view Rabbi Akiva Eiger deranged seduction is rape for all). Especially per Maharik root 168: intent to sin with husband required — if like animal act certainly considered rape; Rambam minors everywhere lack intent — adult teaches minor and seducer is adult-on-minor; deranged certainly does nothing per adulterer since no mind, but like animal act not to sin with husband — permitted also per Rambam (siman 82 letter 7 wanted to innovate per this even per Rambam minor if adulterer younger than nine not adult-on-minor — rejected see there; siman 82 letter 6 even minor if so lacking mind to sin certainly permitted even per Rambam). Further possible even real rape in them no voice; also per Rambam possible establish afterward during madness by real rape (siman 81 letter 1 explained sleep-rape). Perhaps believed per her words per Rabbeinu Yonah as Panim Me'irrot part 3 siman 22 ruled without miggo; Knesset Yechezkel siman above raised without miggo to say raped; Kuntres Acharon on Sefas Yemini permits with plain and presumption of righteousness — all the more here we explained permission without words through presumption of fitness; plainly rely on forbidders with plain and maybe through presumption of righteousness. Further innovation even forbidding Rabbeinu Yonah "I was raped" against majority consent without miggo — here since pregnant, one may say from this itself room to permit: what all poskim dispute applies only saying "I was raped"; but here she on her own claims raped during madness (responsum Pnei Aharon siman there sleep intercourse — see siman 81 letter 1) — possibly more lenient when pregnant, for we rule siman 13 seif 6 like R' Yosi a harlot turns over not to conceive — need not wait three months; also for raped woman this applies ch. 4 Brothers — certainly sleep-rape or during madness no turning-over presumption supporting truth of her words — if not she would turn over; he elaborated (siman 81, 93 on this). Conclusion: permit wife to husband if famous Gaon agrees — he vast pilpul; Gaon Mahari of Lissa questioned some sides of permission but did not overturn his ruling and Gaon Baal Or Chadash wrote to remove doubts and uphold his words; nevertheless after Lissa Gaon did not explicitly agree to permit, he also did not choose alone to permit until he found Noda B'Yehuda simanim 14, 16 that elaborated Rabbeinu Yonah dispute ruling "I was raped" believed — plain helps with presumption of fitness (as brought above s.k. 7); finding one Gaon of the generation to lenient in such case, all the more here better — no majority of consent, also turning-over presumption; he relied to permit (but did not want honor as the permitter — told the questioning husband the Gaon Noda B'Yehuda permits). See also Beit Meir above siman 6 s.k. 14 on this.`,
    "9#_": `He imposes a general cherem, etc. In gloss Shulchan Aruch of Rabbi Akiva Eiger zt"l nb see Ba'er Heitev Sha'ar 26 end ch. 1 who wrote: although we find language "believed" requiring oath such as "she is believed" of R' Gamliel and R' Eliezer ch. 1 Kesubos, etc. — uncertain.`,
    "10#_": `If he wishes to maintain her. Beit Shmuel s.k. 24. It also appears from Tosafot that where it is mistaken transaction he must betroth anew, etc. See responsum Brit Avraham siman 85. And what per Beit Shmuel and Shagas Aryeh that he must divorce her, etc. — see Beit Meir: per our custom making matches with penalties even if he wants to divorce, one may say he is not believed wanting to free her without penalty per Tosafot's view; not so per Rambam and Rosh — one may say nevertheless not believed; but because of the bride's trouble for the feast one may say for all he is not believed. See there.`,
  },
  "069": {
    "1#_": `"I will not work." Ba'er Hetev: from this language it appears, etc., "but what shall I do," etc. See Beit Meir: poskim properly did not distinguish, for otherwise the opening of HaMedir would not be difficult per R' Hoshaya. See there; requires investigation.`,
    "2#_": `"I will complete for you." Ba'er Hetev in name of Beit Shmuel; there he concludes presumably when he is here he also cannot say against her will "go," etc. See Beit Meir who disputes and concludes therefore in my view as long as husband is here none dispute. See there also siman 70 seif 9 on this.`,
    "3#_": `Marital duty. Ba'er Hetev: but with kinyan nothing follows kinyan, Sma siman 22. See Beit Meir who wondered (Sma wrote this on another matter not here) — for marital duty there is no kinyan and it is less than kinyan; regarding ketubah licentious intercourse and inheritance Chelkat Mechokek s.k. 12 wrote even if they acquired from him like inheritance of his father. But see Rashba responsum in Beit Yosef end of siman 118: if they acquired from him to give heirs what she brought if she dies in his lifetime it helps even after marriage — this obligates himself to give, not dependent on inheritance law. Nevertheless Rashba per his view "I give" kinyan helps as explained Beit Yosef Choshen Mishpat siman 295; but for us kinyan must be language of obligation; but removing inheritance is clear even with kinyan ineffective after marriage. See there.`,
  },
  "070": {
    "1#_": `Reduced and had surplus. See responsum Teshuvah Me'ahavah part 1 siman 78 and siman 80 who wrote this is not agreed halakhah — from what they said ch. 1 "even though permitted, sustenance to husband" is no proof, for there they do not discuss reduction but surplus automatically, such as by nature she does not eat as much as Sages allotted average women, as Rashi explained there plainly; plainly surplus belongs to husband since he need not give more than she needs. But in reduction and hunger and hardship she can say she did so for herself not that her husband should benefit; so explicit Tosafot Nazir 24b s.v. shekimetz; Ran Nedarim 88a. Therefore ruled practically not to take from the woman; his teacher Gaon Noda B'Yehuda agreed, writing not because she can say "I hold we are established on it" — for then if she now wants sustenance the husband is presumed and can say the reverse "I hold — go sustain yourself from surplus money in your hand" — but truly this law is clear without doubt. See there at length; likewise in his Dagul MeRevavah on this siman (see there ch. 2 what he wrote on Beit Shmuel s.k. 18). See also Beit Meir here and below siman 80 seif 1; also whether what she earned is nedunya or entirely hers. See there.`,
    "2#_": `And if he was exceedingly poor and cannot, etc. This is Rambam's language ch. 12 Laws of Marriage halakhah 11, and plain language implies he is poor and also cannot hire out, such as ill or constrained — nevertheless they compel divorce. But Shulchan Aruch below siman 154 seif 3 states he has nothing to sustain and does not wish to hire out, etc. — Ran in name of Rashba ch. HaMedir; this language implies he can hire out but does not wish — then they compel; but without ability to hire out being constrained they do not compel — like the first view Rama brought here; therefore Rama did not emend there at all. So explained Beit Meir siman 154; concludes somewhat difficult on Shulchan Aruch that siman 70 plainly like Rambam and here siman 154 like Rashba. See there (see Chasam Sofer siman 131 who also wrote in Shulchan Aruch siman 154 seif 3 manner "does not wish to hire out" — the first view here also agrees they compel divorce, for the first view's reason is no worse than other creditors — even lender dies of hunger if borrower has nothing he is exempt; but when he can hire himself and profit and does not wish — hard to compel him to work and profit when he does not wish — it is livelihood and he is wicked; therefore compel for all; therefore siman 154 seif 3 Rama did not emend (wrote there to resolve Shulchan Aruch contradiction Beit Meir stood on, brought s.k. following). He further wrote what Rambam and Shulchan Aruch "cannot give her," etc. — even if she has from herself and can live on her own money, nevertheless since he has nothing to give, his obligation is livelihood for her, etc. See there). See Noda B'Yehuda second edition siman 90 s.v. what he wrote on this; see above siman 154 s.k. 105.`,
    "3#_": `Some say they do not compel. Beit Meir wrote: were I worthy to decide I would lean per Shulchan Aruch that the first view's reason compares to a creditor claiming "I have nothing to pay" — in my view not comparable, for a loan is declared to him and when he divorces her she marries another who will sustain her — this is in his hand and like his hand to pay. Further this compares to siman 154 seif 7 obligated to divorce even if he also cannot — especially per Rambam sustenance is Torah law like marital duty. See there; he intends to decide like Rambam compel divorce even cannot hire out and is constrained. See Chasam Sofer there citing Beit Meir deciding halakhah like Rambam; author also brought likewise Yeshuot Yaakov. He wrote: what Beit Meir from his own reasoning distinguishes creditor from here — Kesef Mishnah preceded there. Nevertheless one may say the first view's reason: for a loan we hold his body is not collateralized at all as Choshen Mishpat siman 97 seif 15; his wife is like his body and he must sustain her; even vessels they use for marital duty are not seized, etc. Truly one may distinguish: a regular loan is another matter given to divorce her, his body was not collateralized for this only his property; but here sustenance debt is the woman herself since she married him anticipating sustenance — if no sustenance no marriage — properly compel restore woman to her first place meaning divorce her and not be declared to him — imagine one who lent his fellow millstones and wagon and makes his livelihood from them, not pledged as loan but lent; loan term passed — shall we not compel return because he makes livelihood from them, etc. (in my view also included Kesef Mishnah language there: wonder, etc., since one of marriage conditions is sustenance — if he has nothing to sustain, divorce, end quote see there). However one may say this is specifically per Rambam sustenance is Torah law; but per poskim sustenance is rabbinic — marriage and sustenance do not depend so much on each other, etc.; he elaborated somewhat concluding for practical law: one who has and does not wish to sustain — do not compel divorce (only sustain); one who has nothing and does not wish to profit — compel with rods to divorce (as siman 154 seif 3); one who cannot profit being ill and not wicked — depends per Rambam Torah sustenance compel with rods; per not Torah law do not compel with rods; nevertheless with words compel. Thus resolved author's contradiction (Beit Meir in previous s.k.): siman 70 discusses compulsion generally therefore plainly Rambam; siman 154 compulsion with rods like others there therefore wrote Rashba's language. See there also wrote if they transgressed they can compel on the get since through Israel even not according to law valid from Torah per Rambam ch. 2 Laws of Divorce halakhah 20 — thus here cannot profit from illness — per Rambam even ab initio compel; thus for other poskim too if they transgressed and compelled, possibly even rabbinically valid, etc. See there.`,
    "4#_": `Obligated to hire himself out. See responsum Radbaz part 3 siman 566 (see Chasam Sofer siman 132 about a rabbi who went to Israel leaving wife destitute; he commanded giving her chumash from the fund to send him; known insufficient in hard times; questioning Gaon wanted to withhold all fund money by known treasurer and give her — he answered very difficult since initially given only for his sustenance to take him to Israel — cannot change donors' intent; even he himself if wanted to eat it abroad would be thief changing donors' intent, etc. He further wrote nevertheless questioning Gaon's law since clear to us not for her clothing to go there to Israel per Maharai siman 88 and Ma'il Tzedakah siman 26 (brought below siman 75 s.k. 6), etc. — since nevertheless obligated to sustain her, evil and bitter this act to go there and not leave sustenance for his wife — therefore proper to compel in all compulsion ways not in his power to go Israel and starve wife; thus a fortiori that chumash he commanded give — we can give her and it is not changing donors' intent since all donors' intent to maintain him in Holy Land — if wife's sustenance not fixed here, lamentations written upon him forced to uproot and return abroad, etc.; even if seems insufficient for her sustenance we can take from treasurer to give properly, etc.; nevertheless good to warn him first and meanwhile she suffices with chumash he permitted and remainder money held by treasurer until rabbi's answer from there. See there).`,
    "5#_": `Or must sell them. See Chasam Sofer siman 131 on such a case.`,
    "6#_": `And afterward she became deranged. Ba'er Hetev. Beit Shmuel further wrote if she was deranged at marriage and he wrote her ketubah — seems he is obligated for all ketubah conditions, etc. Beit Meir wrote on this: I do not know whence this appears to him that he is obligated for what he did not write, etc. See there.`,
    "7#_": `Wrote the opposite. Ba'er Hetev. Chelkat Mechokek wrote here discusses one who refuses to give ketubah. See Hagahot Yad Efraim: from Rashba's responsum language brought in Darkhei Moshe it is clear even saying "here is her get and ketubah" does not help since he cannot divorce her. See responsum Chut HaShani siman 32 on my case: cannot exempt him because she became deranged — Rambam ch. 10 Laws of Marriage wrote not obligated in her illness; halakhah was decided from mouth of great later authorities like Ra'avad who challenged him and Rashba in responsum obligating in all. Further possible Rambam also agrees since such madness from illness and still in illness — presumed curable, not one in a thousand who cannot be healed; Rambam disputes only where healthy and becomes deranged — that madness is usually permanent and not presumed curable. See there.`,
    "8#_": `One stood on his own. See responsum Rama of Pano siman 89.`,
    "9#_": `And sustained from his own. Ba'er Hetev. See Chut HaShani siman 32: likewise if one stood and redeemed his fellow's wife from captivity the husband need not pay. See there.`,
    "10#_": `However if they were obligated to the husband. From poskim' conclusion it appears even if debt did not yet reach payment time — uncertain.`,
    "11#_": `Only his sustenance. Eglei Tal. However it seems main law one who says "eat with me" must pay him; so too here this law they take from him. See Perishah Choshen Mishpat siman 246 s.k. 17.`,
    "12#_": `She has no sustenance. Ba'er Hetev. See above siman 69 s.k. 2; see also Beit Meir here.`,
  },
};
